---
import Doc from '../../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import Pager from '../../../components/Pager.astro';
const slug = 'getting-started/install';
---
<Doc slug={slug} title="Install">
  <h1>Install</h1>
  <p class="lede">Linux, macOS, and Windows via WSL2. Intel and ARM are supported.</p>

  <h2>Support matrix</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>amd64 / Intel</th><th>arm64 / Apple Silicon</th><th>Recommended path</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Linux</td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td><td>release installer</td></tr>
      <tr><td>macOS</td><td>✓</td><td>✓</td><td>release installer</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Windows 10/11</td><td>✓ via WSL2</td><td>✓ via WSL2</td><td>WSL2 + Linux installer</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Native Windows</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>not currently supported</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Native Windows is intentionally not advertised as supported: Cortex/Axon currently rely on POSIX shell
    execution and process-group semantics. WSL2 provides the supported Linux environment on Windows.
  </p>

  <h2>Linux and macOS <span class="badge">recommended</span></h2>
  <p>The installer detects Linux vs macOS and Intel/AMD64 vs ARM64, installs without root to <code>~/.local/bin</code>, and verifies the binary.</p>
  <pre><code>{`curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/atakang7/cortex/main/scripts/install.sh | sh`}</code></pre>
  <p>Choose another user-owned directory without editing the script:</p>
  <pre><code>{`CORTEX_INSTALL_DIR="$HOME/bin" sh -c 'curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/atakang7/cortex/main/scripts/install.sh | sh'`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Windows 10/11</h2>
  <p>Install WSL2 once from an Administrator PowerShell terminal:</p>
  <pre><code>wsl --install</code></pre>
  <p>Restart if Windows asks, open the installed Linux distribution, then use the same supported installer:</p>
  <pre><code>{`curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/atakang7/cortex/main/scripts/install.sh | sh`}</code></pre>
  <p>Run Cortex from the WSL terminal. Your Windows drives are available under <code>/mnt/c</code>, <code>/mnt/d</code>, and so on.</p>

  <h2>Manual release install</h2>
  <p>For users who do not want to pipe an installer into a shell:</p>
  <pre><code>{`OS=$(uname -s | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')
ARCH=$(uname -m)
case "$ARCH" in x86_64|amd64) ARCH=amd64 ;; arm64|aarch64) ARCH=arm64 ;; *) echo "unsupported architecture: $ARCH"; exit 1 ;; esac
mkdir -p "$HOME/.local/bin"
curl -fL "https://github.com/atakang7/cortex/releases/latest/download/cortex-${OS}-${ARCH}.tar.gz" -o /tmp/cortex.tar.gz
tar -xzf /tmp/cortex.tar.gz -C "$HOME/.local/bin" cortex
chmod 755 "$HOME/.local/bin/cortex"
"$HOME/.local/bin/cortex" --version`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Build from source</h2>
  <p>This works on supported POSIX systems when Go and Git are installed:</p>
  <pre><code>{`git clone https://github.com/atakang7/cortex.git
cd cortex
go build -o cortex ./cmd/cortex
./cortex --version`}</code></pre>

  <h2>About <code>go install</code></h2>
  <p>
    Do not use <code>go install github.com/atakang7/cortex/cmd/cortex@latest</code> for Cortex 2.x.
    The repository's historical module path does not carry the <code>/v2</code> suffix required by
    Go's semantic import versioning, so <code>@latest</code> can resolve the old v1 line. The release installer
    above is the version-safe installation path.
  </p>

  <h2>PATH troubleshooting</h2>
  <p>If installation succeeds but your shell says <code>cortex: command not found</code>, add the user binary directory to PATH:</p>
  <pre><code>{`export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"`}</code></pre>
  <p>Put that line in <code>~/.zshrc</code> (common on macOS) or <code>~/.bashrc</code>/<code>~/.profile</code> (common on Linux/WSL) to keep it across terminals.</p>

  <h2>Verify</h2>
  <pre><code>cortex --version</code></pre>

  <div class="callout info">
    <span class="ico">ℹ️</span>
    <div class="callout-body">
      <strong>What's next?</strong> Cortex needs a model before it can start. Continue to
      <a href="/cortex/docs/getting-started/quick-start/">Quick start</a>.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="docs-pager"><Pager slug={slug} /></div>
</Doc>
